// CIIP Academy question bank, units 1 to 3. All questions are original informatics scenarios.
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u1l1
{id:"u1l1-1",t:"u1l1",q:"A radiologist reports that a chest study is on the modality but never reached the reading worklist. What is the FIRST step in troubleshooting?",a:["Restart the PACS application servers during the next maintenance window","Find the last system that handled the study and check its image count and status","Ask the technologist to repeat the exam so a new study is created","Rebuild the reading worklist filters for every radiologist in the group"],c:1,e:"Workflow troubleshooting follows the study step by step. Identify the last system that saw it, then check that step's owner, because restarting servers or repeating the exam treats a guess, not the break."},
{id:"u1l1-2",t:"u1l1",q:"Which system is the source of truth for the radiology order's status and the scheduling of the exam?",a:["The enterprise viewer","The modality console","The RIS","The speech recognition server"],c:2,e:"The RIS owns scheduling and the radiology order status. The EMR owns the clinician's order and the chart, PACS owns the images, and the reporting system owns the report text."},
{id:"u1l1-3",t:"u1l1",q:"Technologists at one site type patient names into the scanner instead of choosing from a list, and studies keep landing in the unmatched queue. Which workflow step is being skipped?",a:["The result acknowledgment from the EMR","The storage commitment request","The prefetch of relevant priors","The modality worklist query"],c:3,e:"The modality worklist brings the scheduled patient, ID, and accession from the RIS to the scanner. Typing demographics by hand breaks the match to the order, so the study cannot be reconciled."},
{id:"u1l1-4",t:"u1l1",q:"A department wants to reduce turnaround time and proposes buying new workstations. Which action follows the defining, implementing, and evaluating pattern for a workflow solution?",a:["Document the current steps and measure each stage, then design a change that targets the slowest one","Install the new workstations first and survey the readers after three months to see whether they feel faster","Copy the workflow design from a larger hospital and adopt it unchanged","Ask each reader to rank the steps they dislike and fix the top two"],c:0,e:"A workflow solution starts with the current steps and a measured problem, then a design with named owners, a test, go-live, and a repeat measurement. Without the baseline the result cannot be evaluated."},
{id:"u1l1-5",t:"u1l1",q:"Reports are signed in the reporting system, but clinicians never see them in the EMR. Which component is MOST likely to hold the missing messages?",a:["The modality worklist provider that serves the technologists at the scanners","The interface engine queue between the reporting system and the EMR","The storage commitment service on the main archive","The hanging protocol engine on the reading workstations"],c:1,e:"Results travel as messages through the interface engine, which owns the movement between systems. A stalled or errored queue is the first place to look when reports stop appearing in the chart."},

// u1l2
{id:"u1l2-1",t:"u1l2",q:"A vendor says its scanner supports DICOM. What document should you request FIRST to learn which object types and transfer syntaxes it supports?",a:["The hardware installation guide","The product brochure and marketing summary","The DICOM conformance statement","The vendor's service contract template"],c:2,e:"The conformance statement lists the SOP classes, user and provider roles, transfer syntaxes, and options a product implements. It is the document to compare before buying or connecting a device."},
{id:"u1l2-2",t:"u1l2",q:"Two different studies in your archive are found to share one study instance UID. Why is this a serious problem?",a:["The patient ID is deleted whenever two UIDs match each other","UIDs are only used for display, so the studies will look identical to readers","The accession number is replaced by the UID, so billing will fail","UIDs must be globally unique, so the system cannot tell the studies apart and may merge or overwrite objects"],c:3,e:"A UID names exactly one thing and must never repeat for different objects. A duplicate can cause one study to overwrite or be filed with the other, which corrupts the record."},
{id:"u1l2-3",t:"u1l2",q:"A modality sends an image to PACS. Which roles do the two devices play in the storage service?",a:["The modality is the service class user and PACS is the service class provider","The modality is the service class provider and PACS is the service class user","Both devices are service class users for the same association","The roles are reversed whenever the image is compressed"],c:0,e:"The user asks for the service and the provider answers. In a store, the modality asks PACS to accept the object, so the modality is the user and PACS is the provider."},
{id:"u1l2-4",t:"u1l2",q:"An association opens between a scanner and PACS, but PACS refuses the CT image storage presentation context. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The scanner's IP address is wrong","No SOP class and transfer syntax pair is acceptable to both sides","The firewall between the scanner and PACS is dropping every packet it receives","The patient ID is missing from the worklist"],c:1,e:"An opened association proves the title, address, and port are fine. A refused context means the proposed SOP class or the offered transfer syntaxes do not match what the provider accepts."},
{id:"u1l2-5",t:"u1l2",q:"A viewer must find studies for a patient over plain web calls with no DICOM association. Which approach does this?",a:["Storage commitment from the archive to the viewer","A DICOM echo from the viewer to the archive","DICOMweb query and retrieve","A modality performed procedure step message"],c:2,e:"DICOMweb offers query, retrieve, and store over HTTP, so web viewers and apps can reach the archive without opening a traditional DICOM association."},

// u1l3
{id:"u1l3-1",t:"u1l3",q:"New imaging orders from the EMR stop reaching the RIS at 06:40, and no system is down. Where should you look FIRST?",a:["The archive's retention policy","The reading workstation hanging protocols and their prefetch rules","The modality storage commitment settings","The interface engine queue and its acknowledgment errors"],c:3,e:"Orders reach the RIS as messages through the interface engine. A stalled queue or a negative acknowledgment is the most direct explanation when nothing else is down."},
{id:"u1l3-2",t:"u1l3",q:"Which HL7 v2 message type carries the report text and its status from the reporting system to the EMR?",a:["ORU","ORM","ADT","SIU"],c:0,e:"ORU is the unsolicited result message. ORM or OMI carries orders, ADT carries patient events and merges, and SIU carries scheduling."},
{id:"u1l3-3",t:"u1l3",q:"Two patient records are found to belong to one person. Which HL7 message family tells the RIS and PACS that the records are now one?",a:["MDM document messages","ADT merge messages","ORU result messages","SIU appointment messages"],c:1,e:"ADT messages carry registration, updates, and merges. A merge message lets downstream systems combine the records so every system holds the same demographics."},
{id:"u1l3-4",t:"u1l3",q:"A DICOM study cannot be matched to its HL7 order even though the patient is correct. Which value is MOST likely to differ between the two?",a:["The AE title of the scanner","The modality abbreviation","The accession number","The bit depth of the images"],c:2,e:"The accession number ties the ordered exam to the images. If the order and the DICOM header carry different accession numbers, the study cannot reconcile."},
{id:"u1l3-5",t:"u1l3",q:"A patient portal developer wants a simple web interface to read a patient's imaging study list and reports. Which standard is the BEST fit?",a:["A scheduled batch of CSV files","HL7 v2 pipe messages over MLLP","DICOM association with a service class provider","FHIR resources over REST"],c:3,e:"FHIR exposes resources such as ImagingStudy and DiagnosticReport through plain web calls, which suits portals and apps. HL7 v2 over MLLP is built for interface engines inside an organization."},

// u1l4
{id:"u1l4-1",t:"u1l4",q:"Three hospitals in one system use different local names for the same knee exam, and the hanging protocol works at only one of them. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Map each local procedure name to one standard procedure entry and key the protocol on it","Build a separate hanging protocol for every local procedure name","Ask radiologists to rearrange the images by hand each time","Rename the exam at the scanner each morning"],c:0,e:"Mapping local names to one standard set lets protocols, analytics, billing, and exchange all key on the same code. Separate protocols per name multiply maintenance."},
{id:"u1l4-2",t:"u1l4",q:"Which code set is used to describe the procedure performed so it can be billed?",a:["SNOMED CT","CPT","LOINC","ICD"],c:1,e:"CPT names procedures for billing and reporting. ICD describes diagnoses and reasons for the exam, SNOMED CT carries clinical terms, and LOINC names observations and orderables."},
{id:"u1l4-3",t:"u1l4",q:"What does the RadLex Playbook provide?",a:["Billing rates for each imaging procedure","A list of diagnoses for radiology reports","Standard names and codes for imaging procedures","A format for storing studies on portable media and discs"],c:2,e:"The Playbook gives imaging procedures one set of standard names and codes, so each local procedure name can be mapped to it."},
{id:"u1l4-4",t:"u1l4",q:"An analytics report shows the north site doing far fewer knee exams than the east site, though the scanners are similar. What should you verify before acting on the report?",a:["Whether the archive at the east site has more free space","Whether the north site's scanners need new software","Whether the radiologists at the north site read more slowly than those at the east site","Whether every local knee procedure name at the north site is mapped to the same code"],c:3,e:"Counts by procedure are only valid when similar exams share a code. An unmapped local name would be missing from the total and make a site look quieter than it is."},
{id:"u1l4-5",t:"u1l4",q:"Who should own the yearly review of procedure code mapping and retired codes?",a:["A named person in imaging operations with a scheduled review","Whoever notices a failed hanging protocol first","The vendor of the PACS, once a year at renewal","Each technologist for the exams they perform"],c:0,e:"Code sets change every year and local lists grow, so ownership and a schedule are needed. Without them drift is found only when a protocol fails or a claim is denied."},

// u1l5
{id:"u1l5-1",t:"u1l5",q:"A RFP says only that the system shall support IHE. What is the BEST improvement?",a:["Ask the vendor to explain IHE in a paragraph","Name the profiles and actors required and request the integration statement to verify them","Require that the vendor employs an IHE certified engineer on the implementation team for a year","Remove the line and rely on the demonstration"],c:1,e:"Naming the profile and the actor turns a general claim into a testable requirement, and the integration statement shows what the product really supports."},
{id:"u1l5-2",t:"u1l5",q:"A trauma patient is scanned before registration is complete and the images carry a temporary identity. Which IHE profile addresses fixing the demographics afterward?",a:["Key Image Note","Consistent Presentation of Images profile","Patient Information Reconciliation","Portable Data for Imaging"],c:2,e:"Patient Information Reconciliation updates demographics when a patient is imaged before registration is complete, such as in a trauma."},
{id:"u1l5-3",t:"u1l5",q:"Auditors find that timestamps in the audit logs of the PACS and the EMR disagree by several minutes, so events cannot be lined up. Which profile prevents this?",a:["Cross-Enterprise Document Sharing for Imaging","Invoke Image Display","Radiation Exposure Monitoring","Consistent Time"],c:3,e:"Consistent Time has every system synchronize its clock to one source, so audit trails and analytics line up."},
{id:"u1l5-4",t:"u1l5",q:"A vendor's integration statement lists Scheduled Workflow, but only the image display actor. Your RFP required image manager and image archive actors. What is the BEST action?",a:["Score the gap and ask for a written commitment to the missing actors with a date","Accept the statement because Scheduled Workflow is listed","Replace the requirement with the actor the vendor supports","Skip the profile and require only DICOM conformance"],c:0,e:"A profile is only useful for the actors a product claims. A gap found before contract can be closed by a dated commitment or reflected in the evaluation."},
{id:"u1l5-5",t:"u1l5",q:"What is the purpose of an IHE Connectathon?",a:["Hospitals publish their own integration statements to compete for contracts with one another","Vendors test their products against each other's implementations of the profiles","Standards bodies vote on changes to DICOM","Regulators inspect radiation dose records"],c:1,e:"At a Connectathon vendors test interoperability of the profiles with one another. It raises confidence, but acceptance testing on your own network still decides."},

// u1l6
{id:"u1l6-1",t:"u1l6",q:"A clinician clicks a result in the EMR and the viewer opens on a blank page. The archive and network are healthy. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The modality worklist is returning no procedures to the technologist at the scanner","The archive has run out of storage","The launch address carries a patient ID or accession that the viewer cannot match","The speech recognition server has stopped"],c:2,e:"Context launch passes the patient and accession in a web address. If the identifier or its issuer differs from what PACS holds, the viewer finds nothing even though everything else works."},
{id:"u1l6-2",t:"u1l6",q:"Who should receive an enterprise viewer rather than a diagnostic workstation?",a:["Technologists performing acceptance tests","Radiologists reading mammograms","Physicists testing displays","Clinicians outside radiology who need to review images"],c:3,e:"An enterprise or universal viewer serves referring clinicians without forcing them to learn the diagnostic workstation, which is built for interpretation."},
{id:"u1l6-3",t:"u1l6",q:"A dermatology clinic captures photos with a camera and types the patient name each time. What integration change BEST improves patient identity?",a:["Use a worklist or encounter link so the patient is selected, not typed","Ask staff to double check the spelling more carefully","Store the photos on the camera until the end of the day and upload them in a batch","Print each photo and scan it as a document"],c:0,e:"Typed names cause mismatches. A worklist or encounter link brings the patient and procedure from the system of record to the capture device."},
{id:"u1l6-4",t:"u1l6",q:"Which test BEST proves a new EMR to RIS interface is ready for go-live?",a:["One clean order that arrives successfully","Real-looking messages including a merge, a cancel, and a correction, each confirmed in every system","A review of the interface specification by the vendor, followed by a signed statement that it is ready","A ping test between the two servers"],c:1,e:"Interfaces fail on exceptions. Testing merges, cancellations, and corrections, and confirming each result on screen, shows that the whole lifecycle works."},
{id:"u1l6-5",t:"u1l6",q:"Which item is required for every interface before it is considered fully owned?",a:["A separate vendor contract","A dedicated server","A named owner, a test plan, and a downtime procedure","A copy of the interface specification on paper"],c:2,e:"Ownership means a person responsible, a test plan with real messages, and a downtime procedure that has been tried once."},

// u1l7
{id:"u1l7-1",t:"u1l7",q:"A lung CAD tool receives and processes studies, but readers never see its results. The results come back as a new series in a separate study. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Turn off the tool and report it as unsuitable","Buy a faster server for the CAD tool","Ask readers to search for the separate study by hand each time and report the cases to the vendor","Have the tool reuse the original study UID and add a hanging protocol rule for the extra series"],c:3,e:"Results must return to the same study so the reader sees them. A hanging protocol rule then displays the extra series. The algorithm was never the problem."},
{id:"u1l7-2",t:"u1l7",q:"How do computer-aided detection results usually travel back to PACS?",a:["As a DICOM structured report or a secondary capture image","As an e-mail attachment sent to each radiologist who requested the study","As a spreadsheet saved on a file share","As an HL7 order message"],c:0,e:"CAD marks are stored in the DICOM world as a structured report or a secondary capture image, linked to the study."},
{id:"u1l7-3",t:"u1l7",q:"Where does clinical decision support act at order entry?",a:["It chooses the display layout for the reader","It checks whether the requested exam is appropriate and can suggest another","It stores the finished report","It deletes duplicate studies"],c:1,e:"At order entry decision support evaluates appropriateness of the exam for the clinical question. It also works at protocoling and during interpretation."},
{id:"u1l7-4",t:"u1l7",q:"A dose registry wants exposure data from every scanner. What should each modality send?",a:["A summary typed by the technologist after each exam","A screenshot of the console","A dose structured report at the end of the exam","The first image of each series"],c:2,e:"Modalities emit a dose structured report that the registry reads to compare exams against reference levels and to flag outliers."},
{id:"u1l7-5",t:"u1l7",q:"For a new artificial intelligence tool, what is the MOST important integration question?",a:["Whether the vendor publishes its source code","How large its logo is on the screen","Whether it runs on the same brand of server and storage as the PACS archive","Where its input comes from and where its output is stored and shown"],c:3,e:"An AI tool is another consumer of images and producer of results, so it needs a route in, a route out, and a place in the worklist or the viewer."},

// u1l8
{id:"u1l8-1",t:"u1l8",q:"A critical result log shows only 'message sent' for a finding reported overnight. What is missing from the closed loop?",a:["Confirmation by the responsible clinician, with name and time","The radiologist's signature on the report","A copy to the quality committee","An image key note on the study"],c:0,e:"A closed loop records who was told, by whom, and when, and the clinician's acknowledgment. Sending a message is not confirming it."},
{id:"u1l8-2",t:"u1l8",q:"A resident's preliminary read differs from the attending's final read in a way that changes management. What should the workflow ensure?",a:["Only the final report is stored","The ordering clinician is told of the discordant finding","The preliminary report is deleted","The resident is notified by e-mail and the attending is told at the next meeting"],c:1,e:"Discordant findings must reach the clinician who acted on the preliminary read. Informatics supports it with a status flow and an alert."},
{id:"u1l8-3",t:"u1l8",q:"Turnaround analytics shows some reports signed before their exams were complete. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The reporting system deletes old studies","The radiologists sign reports before reading","The systems use different clocks, so timestamps do not line up","The scanners compress the images too aggressively before sending them"],c:2,e:"Negative times come from unsynchronized clocks. One time source for every system, as in Consistent Time, fixes it."},
{id:"u1l8-4",t:"u1l8",q:"Which organization is the informatics society whose work, with ACR and AAPM, shapes imaging informatics practice?",a:["HITECH","ABII","The Joint Commission","SIIM"],c:3,e:"SIIM is the society for imaging informatics. ABII awards the credential, and The Joint Commission accredits organizations."},
{id:"u1l8-5",t:"u1l8",q:"A hospital plans a new outpatient imaging site in eighteen months. How should the image management strategy respond?",a:["Align network, storage, licenses, and staffing with that plan on a dated schedule","Wait until the site opens and then order equipment","Ask the vendor to size the site without a plan","Postpone upgrades at all existing sites"],c:0,e:"Strategy follows the organization's strategic plan. Growth decides what to build and when, so infrastructure is scheduled with the plan, not after it."},

// u2l1
{id:"u2l1-1",t:"u2l1",q:"A new multi-slice scanner doubles the daily storage growth. Which two checks should come FIRST?",a:["The color depth of the technologist console","The slice count per study and whether thin and thick reconstructions are both sent to PACS","The brand and model of the display on the reading workstation and the length of its warranty","The AE title length of the scanner"],c:1,e:"CT size is matrix times bytes per pixel times slice count, so thin-slice reconstructions and duplicate series multiply storage. Check those before looking at anything else."},
{id:"u2l1-2",t:"u2l1",q:"A CT image has a 512 by 512 matrix at 2 bytes per pixel. What is the size of one slice?",a:["1,048,576 bytes","262,144 bytes","524,288 bytes","786,432 bytes"],c:2,e:"512 times 512 is 262,144 pixels, and at 2 bytes each the slice is 524,288 bytes. Times 500 slices gives about 262 MB, the sheet's average for a multi-slice study."},
{id:"u2l1-3",t:"u2l1",q:"Which statement about mammography data is correct?",a:["Mammography priors are never used for comparison","Mammograms are small and can share a standard display","Mammography studies are always lossy compressed on arrival so that they fit on a standard display","Lossy compressed copies cannot serve as the legal record, and the study needs specialty display"],c:3,e:"Mammography studies are large (a 4 image screening study is about 197 MB uncompressed), need specialty displays, are compared with priors, and lossy copies cannot be the legal record."},
{id:"u2l1-4",t:"u2l1",q:"An ultrasound scanner is not connected to the worklist and technologists type patient data. What risk does this create?",a:["Wrong or misspelled demographics that prevent matching to the order","Larger images that fill the archive","Loss of the cine loop frames","Lower display contrast on the scanner"],c:0,e:"Hand entry produces mismatches with the order and the patient record. Using the worklist protects the link."},
{id:"u2l1-5",t:"u2l1",q:"Computed radiography uses cassettes. What step is specific to that workflow compared with a digital detector?",a:["A change in the DICOM port","An identification step that matches the cassette to the patient","A different transfer syntax for every image","Automatic deletion of the images from the plate after the technologist reads them"],c:1,e:"A cassette must be matched to the patient at an ID station, and a wrong match is a classic wrong-patient error. A digital panel captures directly into the exam."},

// u2l2
{id:"u2l2-1",t:"u2l2",q:"A PET and CT exam arrives as two studies under slightly different patient names, and fusion fails. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The archive uses a different transfer syntax for PET images than for CT images","The scanner has too little memory","The PET operator typed the name while the CT came from the worklist","The CT slices are too thin"],c:2,e:"Both parts of a PET and CT exam must carry the same patient identity. Typing one and using the worklist for the other creates a mismatch that stops fusion."},
{id:"u2l2-2",t:"u2l2",q:"Which department system most often holds echo loops and waveform objects outside the main PACS?",a:["The nuclear medicine workstation","The visible light capture device","The ultrasound scanner at the bedside","The cardiology system"],c:3,e:"Cardiology often runs a separate department system and archive for echo, cath lab, and waveforms. It must be connected to the enterprise so priors and reports are shared."},
{id:"u2l2-3",t:"u2l2",q:"Radiation therapy plans are stored in PACS but the oncology system cannot display them. What should you check?",a:["That the plan, structure set, and dose objects reference the same planning study UID","That the monitors are calibrated","That the images are lossless compressed","That the accession number is 12 digits long"],c:0,e:"RT objects link to the planning images by UID, and the oncology system, not PACS alone, consumes them. A mismatch in references breaks the link."},
{id:"u2l2-4",t:"u2l2",q:"Endoscopy video and photos are captured outside radiology workflows. What problem is MOST common?",a:["The image matrix is too small","The order or patient link is missing","The video cannot be stored in any format","The radiologists dislike the viewer"],c:1,e:"Visible light capture often has no order, so the patient link must come from a worklist, a barcode, or an encounter list."},
{id:"u2l2-5",t:"u2l2",q:"Why does digital pathology need separate storage planning?",a:["They must always be stored on removable media","The images are always lossy","Whole-slide images are very large objects","They have no patient identity"],c:2,e:"Whole-slide images are among the largest objects in the enterprise, so storage and viewing must be planned for them separately."},

// u2l3
{id:"u2l3-1",t:"u2l3",q:"A new scanner's C-ECHO to PACS is rejected. The scanner title was typed in capitals but PACS lists it in lowercase. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Restart the scanner and repeat the echo","Open more firewall ports between the two devices on the network","Change the scanner's IP address","Correct the AE title on PACS to match the scanner exactly"],c:3,e:"AE titles are case-sensitive. A rejected association means the connection reached PACS, so the title on its allowed list must match exactly."},
{id:"u2l3-2",t:"u2l3",q:"A modality shows 'connection refused' when it sends to PACS. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The port is wrong or the receiving service is stopped","The AE title is in the wrong case","A firewall is silently dropping the packets","The patient ID is missing from the study"],c:0,e:"Refused means something answered and said no: a wrong port or a service that is not listening. A timeout with no answer points to a firewall."},
{id:"u2l3-3",t:"u2l3",q:"A technologist asks why the scanner should not delete images as soon as they are sent. What is the correct answer?",a:["Images are deleted automatically after 24 hours, so the scanner never needs a commitment","Wait for storage commitment, which confirms PACS took responsibility for the objects","Deleting is a decision for the radiologist","Deleting is allowed once the C-ECHO succeeds"],c:1,e:"Only after storage commitment may the modality purge its copy. A successful echo or a sent image does not prove PACS has stored them."},
{id:"u2l3-4",t:"u2l3",q:"What does a performed procedure step message with status completed tell RIS and PACS?",a:["The radiologist has signed the report of record","The patient has been billed","The exam has ended and which images were made","The images have been compressed"],c:2,e:"MPPS reports in progress when the exam starts and completed with the images made, so status can update and a missing image can be noticed."},
{id:"u2l3-5",t:"u2l3",q:"A C-ECHO succeeds from the scanner to PACS, but test images are not stored. What does the echo NOT prove?",a:["That the port is open","That the AE titles match","That the address is correct","That the SOP class and transfer syntax will be accepted for storing images"],c:3,e:"An echo proves titles, address, and port match and an association opens. It does not prove that the storage SOP class or a transfer syntax will be accepted."},

// u2l4
{id:"u2l4-1",t:"u2l4",q:"A scanner offers CT image storage in JPEG 2000 only, while PACS accepts only little endian syntaxes. What happens?",a:["The context is rejected and the images are not sent","PACS converts the images silently","The scanner chooses the PACS syntax","The association is released with a success status for every image"],c:0,e:"If no proposed syntax is acceptable the presentation context is rejected and the objects are not sent. One side must enable a common syntax."},
{id:"u2l4-2",t:"u2l4",q:"Which transfer syntax must every DICOM device support?",a:["JPEG baseline lossy syntax","Implicit VR little endian","JPEG 2000","RLE lossless"],c:1,e:"Implicit VR little endian is the default every device must support, which is why it is the fallback when nothing else is agreed."},
{id:"u2l4-3",t:"u2l4",q:"An imported study behaves oddly in the viewer. Which tool BEST checks whether the object breaks the standard?",a:["A disk usage report","A network ping","A DICOM validator","A display photometer"],c:2,e:"A validator checks required elements, value types, and UIDs against the standard and points to the malformed attribute."},
{id:"u2l4-4",t:"u2l4",q:"What does a secondary capture object store?",a:["A recording of the association","The original raw detector data","A copy of the report text","A screenshot or derived picture wrapped as a DICOM image"],c:3,e:"A secondary capture wraps a picture, such as a screenshot, as a DICOM image. It has no original pixel data to remeasure."},
{id:"u2l4-5",t:"u2l4",q:"Association rejected points to which problem, compared with association accepted but images refused?",a:["An unknown AE title, versus a SOP class or transfer syntax mismatch","A firewall, versus a disk failure","A bad cable, versus an expired license","A wrong accession number, versus a wrong patient name on the order form"],c:0,e:"Rejection at association time means the called or calling title is not allowed. If the association opens but data is refused, the negotiation of the SOP class or syntax failed."},

// u2l5
{id:"u2l5-1",t:"u2l5",q:"A reader says the second diagnostic display looks washed out, but the images are fine. What is the BEST first action?",a:["Adjust the brightness by eye until it looks right to the reader and leave it there","Measure the display with a test pattern and a meter against the calibration limits","Replace the display immediately","Move the display to another room"],c:1,e:"A display can drift with no visible fault. Test pattern plus meter is the evidence, and the result is recorded."},
{id:"u2l5-2",t:"u2l5",q:"A diagnostic display reads a maximum of 380 and a minimum of 1.2 cd/m2. What is its contrast ratio and does it meet the diagnostic limit of above 250?",a:["About 456, which does not meet it","About 3.2, which does not meet it","About 317, which meets it","About 78, which meets it"],c:2,e:"Contrast ratio is maximum divided by minimum: 380 divided by 1.2 is about 317, above the 250 required of a diagnostic display."},
{id:"u2l5-3",t:"u2l5",q:"Two displays at one workstation have centers that differ by 12 percent. What is the result?",a:["They fail only if one is a clinical display","They pass because the limit is 30 percent","They pass because both are diagnostic","They exceed the 10 percent allowance, so one should be recalibrated"],c:3,e:"The centers of matched displays should not differ by more than 10 percent. The 30 percent figure is the limit for uniformity within a single display."},
{id:"u2l5-4",t:"u2l5",q:"Who must evaluate a mammography display before clinical use?",a:["A qualified medical physicist","The PACS vendor's sales engineer","The radiologist's assistant","Any trained IT technician"],c:0,e:"Mammography displays require a qualified medical physicist to evaluate them before clinical use, with records kept by the PACS team."},
{id:"u2l5-5",t:"u2l5",q:"What is the difference between an acceptance test and a routine test?",a:["Acceptance is done monthly, routine once","Acceptance uses the full pattern set once at installation, routine uses simpler checks on a schedule","Acceptance is only for mammography displays","Routine tests replace the need for any records, because the display already passed its acceptance test"],c:1,e:"The acceptance test is a complete one-time set of patterns and measurements. Routine checks follow on a schedule with simpler tests and a visual pattern."},

// u2l6
{id:"u2l6-1",t:"u2l6",q:"A keyboard must be replaced inside the MRI control area during a busy day. What is the correct approach?",a:["Send the keyboard in with the patient","Enter quickly with the tool bag because the request is urgent and the scanner is idle","Call MRI staff, complete screening, leave steel tools outside, and be escorted in","Wait until the end of the day and ignore the screening step"],c:2,e:"The magnet is always on. Screening and an escort come first, and only equipment labeled for the area may enter."},
{id:"u2l6-2",t:"u2l6",q:"Which action reduces exposure when you must work near an x-ray room?",a:["Ignore exposure because informatics staff are not clinicians","Stay close to the source to finish faster","Work with the door open for ventilation","Keep time short, stay as far away as the task allows, and use the shielding provided"],c:3,e:"Time, distance, and shielding control exposure. Ask the technologist before entering a room where an exam may be under way."},
{id:"u2l6-3",t:"u2l6",q:"What is the correct behavior near the sterile field in an operating room?",a:["Stay out of it unless gowned and invited","Lean over it to reach a cable","Place a laptop on its edge","Touch only the items that look clean to you"],c:0,e:"The sterile field belongs to the surgical team. Do not enter, lean over, or touch what is on it."},
{id:"u2l6-4",t:"u2l6",q:"A powered cart has a damaged power cord. What should you do?",a:["Tape the cord and use the cart","Report it and keep the device out of use","Open the housing to find the fault","Plug the cart into a different outlet and use it"],c:1,e:"Do not open powered equipment or use damaged cords. Report the fault so qualified staff can repair it."},
{id:"u2l6-5",t:"u2l6",q:"You are unsure whether a room is safe to enter. What is the BEST action?",a:["Enter only when you are carrying a badge","Enter and observe the other staff","Ask the department safety officer or lead technologist before entering","Wait for the patient to leave and then enter"],c:2,e:"When unsure, ask first. The habit of asking before entering an unfamiliar room matters more than knowing every hazard."},

// u3l1
{id:"u3l1-1",t:"u3l1",q:"A reader on the end workstation says dark images look flat in the afternoon, but the display's calibration report is in tolerance. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["An outdated hanging protocol that applies the wrong window and level","A failed backlight in the display that the photometer has not yet detected","A wrong transfer syntax on the images that makes them look flat","Afternoon sunlight raising the ambient light and reflecting in the screen"],c:3,e:"Ambient light above the 20 to 40 lux range lowers perceived contrast, and reflections add glare. Fix the room with blinds and dimmable lighting rather than recalibrating the display."},
{id:"u3l1-2",t:"u3l1",q:"What ambient light range is recommended for image interpretation?",a:["20 to 40 lux","100 to 150 lux","0 lux in total darkness","300 to 500 lux"],c:0,e:"Dim, even, controllable light at 20 to 40 lux avoids reflections yet lets the eye adapt to the surroundings and the displays."},
{id:"u3l1-3",t:"u3l1",q:"Three readers report neck strain after a new room opens. Which design control addresses repetitive strain BEST?",a:["One fixed desk height chosen as the best compromise for every reader in the room","Adjustable chairs and desks with matched monitors at a comfortable height","Larger fonts on the worklist so readers lean in less often","Fewer breaks so the worklist keeps moving through the long afternoon"],c:1,e:"Adjustability is the control for strain from fixed postures: furniture that changes height and screens and devices placed where the body rests naturally."},
{id:"u3l1-4",t:"u3l1",q:"Where should speech recognition users be placed in a shared reading room?",a:["Next to the consult space so that colleagues can ask questions","Seated side by side at a single shared desk for convenience","Separated from each other with sound absorption","Facing a bright window so the room stays well lit"],c:2,e:"Dictation noise disturbs neighbors. Absorb sound with panels and carpet, and separate speech users from each other."},
{id:"u3l1-5",t:"u3l1",q:"A room plan puts twelve workstations in a space with no extra cooling. What problem is MOST likely?",a:["Loss of display calibration data in the workstation memory","Increased network latency between the workstations and the archive","Reduced image matrix on every display in the room","Heat from the computers making the room uncomfortable"],c:3,e:"Many computers warm a room, so cooling and airflow must be planned for every workstation. Involve facilities early."},

// u3l2
{id:"u3l2-1",t:"u3l2",q:"Radiologists log into the reporting tool, the EMR, and PACS separately for each study. Which change BEST reduces the effort?",a:["Context synchronization with single sign-on","A larger monitor on every workstation to show all three applications","A second reporting tool installed beside the first one","More frequent password changes so each login feels more deliberate"],c:0,e:"Context synchronization moves every application to the same patient when a study opens, and single sign-on removes repeated logins. The saving repeats for every study."},
{id:"u3l2-2",t:"u3l2",q:"A radiologist says the system is slow. What should informatics do FIRST?",a:["Order faster workstations for the whole reading room","Measure which step is slow, how long it takes, and how often","Replace the network switch nearest to the reading room this quarter","Reduce the study volume sent to that reader"],c:1,e:"A complaint needs a measurement. A log shows whether the delay is study opening, the reporting tool, or the login, so the fix matches the cause."},
{id:"u3l2-3",t:"u3l2",q:"What is the difference in contrast ratio requirements between diagnostic and clinical review displays?",a:["Both must be above 500 so that every display matches","Above 100 for diagnostic and above 250 for clinical","Above 250 for diagnostic and above 100 for clinical","There is no requirement for either type of display"],c:2,e:"Diagnostic displays need a contrast ratio above 250, and clinical review displays above 100, reflecting their different uses."},
{id:"u3l2-4",t:"u3l2",q:"What does a programmable keypad improve in the reading workflow?",a:["It removes the need for a worklist at the workstation","It raises the display's maximum luminance through the driver","It converts grayscale images to color during review","It maps frequent actions such as window and level to single buttons"],c:3,e:"A keypad saves a click on actions repeated hundreds of times a day, which adds up to measurable time."},
{id:"u3l2-5",t:"u3l2",q:"Which BEST describes the human-computer interface in imaging?",a:["The integration of EMR, RIS, PACS, and reporting with input devices and displays","The video cable between each display and its workstation","The password policy and session timeout rules that govern the reading workstations","The brand and model of the diagnostic monitor in use"],c:0,e:"The interface covers how the systems work together on one desktop, along with the input devices and displays."},

// u3l3
{id:"u3l3-1",t:"u3l3",q:"Readers rearrange knee studies by hand every Monday. A header check shows three scanners label the same series with different names. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Ask the readers to relabel each series themselves before they begin each read and save the change","Standardize the series descriptions or add all names to the rule, and map the procedure code","Replace the hanging protocol engine with one from another vendor","Reduce the number of scanners that send knee studies to PACS"],c:1,e:"Protocols match on values in the study. Inconsistent series descriptions make the rule misfire, so standardize them or include all variants, and test with one study from each scanner."},
{id:"u3l3-2",t:"u3l3",q:"Which condition defines a read-ready study?",a:["The study is older than one hour and the images are all stored","The technologist has left the department and closed the exam","All images arrived, patient and order are matched, and priors are prefetched","The report has already been dictated by the first reader"],c:2,e:"Read-ready means complete, correct, and with its priors ready, so the reader can open it and begin."},
{id:"u3l3-3",t:"u3l3",q:"A reader marks a lesion with an arrow. How should the mark be stored?",a:["Inside the file name of the study so it can be searched","By changing the original pixels so the arrow becomes a permanent part of the image","As a paper printout placed in the patient's folder","As a presentation state or key image note that points at the original image"],c:3,e:"Annotations are stored as their own objects, so the original pixel data never changes."},
{id:"u3l3-4",t:"u3l3",q:"A relevant-prior rule fetches every study for the patient, any modality, any date, with no limit. What problem is MOST likely?",a:["Readers wait for many irrelevant studies","No priors are ever found for any patient","The studies are lossy compressed on retrieval","The wrong patient is chosen for the comparison"],c:0,e:"Too wide a rule brings irrelevant studies and delays opening. Too narrow a rule finds none. Review the rule against complaints."},
{id:"u3l3-5",t:"u3l3",q:"What does window and level change?",a:["The number of slices stored in the study","The contrast and brightness of the displayed image","The patient's demographics in the header","The transfer syntax used to store the file on the archive"],c:1,e:"Window and level adjust how pixel values map to displayed contrast and brightness. They do not change stored pixel data."},

// u3l4
{id:"u3l4-1",t:"u3l4",q:"A 262 MB CT study is stored with lossless compression at 2.5 to 1. What is the compressed size?",a:["26.2 MB","655 MB","104.8 MB","131 MB"],c:2,e:"Compressed size equals original divided by the ratio: 262 divided by 2.5 is 104.8 MB, a saving of 157.2 MB."},
{id:"u3l4-2",t:"u3l4",q:"IT proposes lossy compression for the primary archive to save space. Who must decide?",a:["The finance department, because the savings are financial and appear in its budget","IT alone, since IT owns the storage and its budget","The PACS vendor, because it knows the product best","Radiology leadership under policy, with the ratio recorded on each image"],c:3,e:"The radiology department and its policies decide what may be lossy compressed for interpretation, and the ratio must be stated on each image of the study."},
{id:"u3l4-3",t:"u3l4",q:"Which use is a good fit for lossy compression?",a:["A mobile or web review copy","The legal record of a mammogram","A primary archive copy of every study","A source for measurement in research"],c:0,e:"Lossy compression suits review copies and previews, where small files load fast. Mammography legal records must never be lossy."},
{id:"u3l4-4",t:"u3l4",q:"Which transfer syntax is lossy?",a:["JPEG lossless","JPEG baseline","RLE lossless","JPEG 2000 lossless only"],c:1,e:"JPEG baseline discards data. JPEG lossless, RLE lossless, and the lossless-only JPEG 2000 syntax recover every pixel, and JPEG 2000 may be either."},
{id:"u3l4-5",t:"u3l4",q:"A department stores 40 CT studies a day at about 262 MB each. Which daily total results after lossless 2.5 to 1 compression?",a:["1,048 MB","10,480 MB","4,192 MB","26,200 MB"],c:2,e:"40 times 262 is 10,480 MB uncompressed. Divided by 2.5 gives 4,192 MB a day."},

// u3l5
{id:"u3l5-1",t:"u3l5",q:"A wrong-patient chest study is found in the unverified queue. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Merge it into the other patient's record to remove the duplicate","Delete the study at once so that nobody can open it","Wait for the radiologist to notice and report the problem","Flag it and stop it from being distributed or read"],c:3,e:"First prevent harm: flag the study and stop its distribution. Then check whether it was viewed or reported, confirm the true patient, move it, and write the audit entry."},
{id:"u3l5-2",t:"u3l5",q:"One study sits under the wrong patient while the correct patient has no images. Which correction applies?",a:["Move the study to the correct patient","Split the wrong patient's record in two","Merge the two patients into one record","Delete the study and re-acquire the exam"],c:0,e:"A move relocates a study to the correct patient. A merge combines two records for one patient, and a split separates one record that holds two patients."},
{id:"u3l5-3",t:"u3l5",q:"The technologist acquired 3 images but PACS shows 2, and no storage commitment arrived. What is the correct step?",a:["Delete the study and re-acquire the whole exam","Resend the missing image from the modality","Accept the study as complete and close the exam","Delete the modality's copy to free the local disk"],c:1,e:"The modality still holds the third image because commitment has not arrived. Resend it, since re-acquiring exposes the patient to more radiation."},
{id:"u3l5-4",t:"u3l5",q:"Who may perform merges, splits, and moves, and how are they recorded?",a:["Radiologists only, with the change agreed verbally at the time and noted later in an e-mail","Any technologist on shift, with no record kept of the change","Trained and authorized staff, with each change in the audit trail showing who and why","Vendors, through remote sessions that are not logged"],c:2,e:"These actions change the record, so only trained authorized staff may do them, and each is logged with who and why."},
{id:"u3l5-5",t:"u3l5",q:"What is the purpose of order reconciliation?",a:["Selecting the hanging protocol for each exam","Compressing studies to save archive space","Calibrating displays against the grayscale function","Matching each study to its order using the accession number"],c:3,e:"Reconciliation links each study to its order by accession number, which catches studies with mistyped or missing demographics."},

// u3l6
{id:"u3l6-1",t:"u3l6",q:"A report is changed after it is signed final. How should it appear to readers?",a:["As an addended or corrected report, flagged, with the original text kept","As a silent edit of the final text with no new version","As a new preliminary report that quietly replaces the signed one in the chart","It should be deleted and created again from nothing"],c:0,e:"A change after final is an addendum or a correction, never a silent edit, and readers must see its status."},
{id:"u3l6-2",t:"u3l6",q:"An emergency physician read a preliminary report, but the EMR still shows it after the final was signed. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The final report was never dictated by the radiologist","The EMR interface stores only the first result for an order","The images were lossy compressed before the report was signed","The study was not committed to the archive in time"],c:1,e:"The reporting system sent the final message, but the EMR did not replace the earlier version. The interface must add or replace a version with its status shown."},
{id:"u3l6-3",t:"u3l6",q:"Which report style is BEST for counting findings and feeding decision support?",a:["Handwritten notes","Narrative reporting by free text","Structured reporting","Verbal reporting"],c:2,e:"Structured reports use standard fields and terms, so they are easier to search and count."},
{id:"u3l6-4",t:"u3l6",q:"Which is a typical speech recognition error that can change a report's meaning?",a:["A slower opening of the study","A change in the font of the report","A longer file name for the saved report","A missing negative or a wrong side"],c:3,e:"Speech recognition can drop a negative, swap left and right, or use a sound-alike word, so the author must read the draft before signing."},
{id:"u3l6-5",t:"u3l6",q:"Which record shows who knew what and when about a report?",a:["The audit trail of status changes and views","The hanging protocol rules for the exam","The modality worklist entry for the exam","The storage commitment record from the modality"],c:0,e:"Every view and status change should be logged with user and time, so the sequence can be reconstructed."},

// u3l7
{id:"u3l7-1",t:"u3l7",q:"After header cleaning, a CT localizer image shows the patient's name in the corner. Why was it missed?",a:["The UIDs were not replaced during the cleaning job","The text is burned into the pixel data, which header cleaning does not touch","The study was too large for the cleaning tool to read","The viewer cached the old header from the first view"],c:1,e:"Burned-in annotations live in the pixels and need pixel-level masking or removal."},
{id:"u3l7-2",t:"u3l7",q:"How should UIDs be handled during de-identification?",a:["Keep the original UIDs so the images can always be traced back to the source","Delete them from every image in the study","Replace them consistently so the study keeps its own structure","Set them all to the same value for simplicity"],c:2,e:"Replace each UID consistently so every image in a series shares the same new series UID. Identical UIDs for different objects would break uniqueness."},
{id:"u3l7-3",t:"u3l7",q:"What is pseudonymization?",a:["Compressing the headers so the identifiers cannot be read","Deleting all identifiers permanently so that nobody, even the research team, can restore them","Encrypting the whole archive with a single shared password","Replacing identifiers with a code and keeping a separate key to restore the link"],c:3,e:"A code stands in for identity and a separately held key lets authorized people restore the link, for example for a follow-up scan."},
{id:"u3l7-4",t:"u3l7",q:"Which risk is specific to head CT and MRI volumes in de-identified research data?",a:["The volume can be rendered into a recognizable face","The files become lossy when they are exported","The studies lose their UIDs during the export","The matrix doubles in size after cleaning"],c:0,e:"Three-dimensional reconstruction can show a recognizable face, so facial features may need to be removed or limited."},
{id:"u3l7-5",t:"u3l7",q:"What should happen before de-identified studies leave the organization?",a:["The studies are sent first and checked afterward by the recipient","A header dump, a private tag check, and a sample image review","Only the file names are checked for patient names and dates","Nothing more, because the header cleaning job ran without errors"],c:1,e:"Treat the output as untrusted until checked. A header dump, private tag check, and a look at sample images catch what the job missed."},

// u3l8
{id:"u3l8-1",t:"u3l8",q:"Stage times show read-ready to preliminary as the longest wait. What does the analysis suggest to do FIRST?",a:["Reduce the archive size to speed up retrieval","Replace the modalities with faster scanners","Focus on that stage, for example by balancing the worklist","Shorten the registration process at the front desk before the exam starts"],c:2,e:"The longest stage is the bottleneck, so effort there pays most. Worklist balancing is a common fix."},
{id:"u3l8-2",t:"u3l8",q:"Chest readers sit idle while musculoskeletal studies pile up. Which solution fits BEST?",a:["Lengthening the shifts of the musculoskeletal readers","Adding more monitors to each reading workstation","Stopping subspecialty reads and assigning all studies to readers at random","Worklist balancing with priority rules that keep stat studies first"],c:3,e:"Balancing routes work by subspecialty, priority, and workload, with stat studies still first."},
{id:"u3l8-3",t:"u3l8",q:"A home reader says studies open slowly. What should be checked FIRST?",a:["Retrieval time and bandwidth for a large study","The reader's screen brightness and calibration date","The worklist sort order and its filters","The report template and its macros"],c:0,e:"Slow opening is a network or prefetch problem. Measure retrieval time and bandwidth before changing the worklist."},
{id:"u3l8-4",t:"u3l8",q:"Which is required for a remote reader's display?",a:["No special requirements because the reader is off site","It must meet the same quality rules as the reading room","Only a color display of any size and brand","A photograph of the last calibration report"],c:1,e:"Remote reading should meet the same display quality requirements as the reading room, with calibration and acceptance testing."},
{id:"u3l8-5",t:"u3l8",q:"Which is the BEST security practice for remote reading?",a:["Storing studies on home computers so they open faster","One shared account for all remote readers to ease support and speed up their first login","Encrypted access with strong sign-in, logging, and no images saved on personal devices","Turning off the audit log to protect reader privacy"],c:2,e:"Remote access should use an encrypted connection, strong authentication, logging, and no local copies."},

// u3l9
{id:"u3l9-1",t:"u3l9",q:"A disc arrives carrying 'Robert Lee' with a different birth date than your record for 'Bob Lee.' What should you do?",a:["Refuse every outside disc on principle","Import it under the closest matching name and fix it later","Import it under both names to be safe","Verify identity, correct the demographics, and then import"],c:3,e:"Never import on a guess. An unreconciled study can be filed in the wrong chart and read as a prior."},
{id:"u3l9-2",t:"u3l9",q:"What is the standard layout for studies on portable media?",a:["PDI with a DICOMDIR index and often a viewer","A zip archive of files named by date","A single very large image of the study","A spreadsheet listing each study and its owner"],c:0,e:"Portable Data for Imaging defines the layout with a DICOMDIR index and commonly a viewer."},
{id:"u3l9-3",t:"u3l9",q:"Which standard lets enterprises publish and fetch imaging documents with a registry, repository, and manifest?",a:["PDI","XDS-I.b","HL7 ADT","SIU"],c:1,e:"XDS-I.b applies cross-enterprise document sharing to imaging, with a manifest that points at the images."},
{id:"u3l9-4",t:"u3l9",q:"Which is a typical risk of an imported study?",a:["Excess storage commitment from the sender","Too much calibration on the sending side","Wrong demographics, duplicate UIDs, unknown transfer syntaxes, and corrupt files","A missing keyboard or mouse on the workstation that performs the import for the department"],c:2,e:"Imported studies come from outside systems, so identity, UID, syntax, and file integrity all need checking."},
{id:"u3l9-5",t:"u3l9",q:"Who sets the rules for who may export images and how it is logged?",a:["Each technologist, according to the situation","The PACS administrator alone, as part of the daily work","The vendor, in the support contract for the archive","An exchange policy owned by compliance and radiology"],c:3,e:"Policy decides who may export, what, and how it is tracked. It is owned by compliance and radiology, not by IT alone."},

// u3l10
{id:"u3l10-1",t:"u3l10",q:"The archive is growing. Who decides what may be deleted?",a:["A named policy owner working with compliance and legal","The PACS administrator, who runs the archive every day","The storage vendor, based on the size of the contract","Any radiologist who finds a study that is no longer needed"],c:0,e:"IT carries out deletion but does not decide it. A policy owner sets the schedule within state and legal rules."},
{id:"u3l10-2",t:"u3l10",q:"What is the minimum retention for a mammogram when the patient has no later mammogram at the facility?",a:["1 year, after which the facility may delete it","10 years, and longer if a state requires it","5 years in every case, whatever happens later","Until the next annual screening is performed"],c:1,e:"Mammography is kept at least 5 years, or 10 years when there is no later mammogram at the facility, and longer where a state requires it."},
{id:"u3l10-3",t:"u3l10",q:"What does a legal hold do?",a:["Moves the study to portable media for safekeeping","Compresses the study to save archive space","Suspends deletion of any record involved in a case","Changes the study's UID so it cannot be altered"],c:2,e:"A legal hold overrides the retention schedule for records involved in a case until it is lifted."},
{id:"u3l10-4",t:"u3l10",q:"Which record must be kept for legal retention?",a:["A screenshot","A digitized film copy made from the original film","A lossy review copy","The original from which the diagnosis was made"],c:3,e:"The original is the record. A digitized film copy is not the original for legal retention."},
{id:"u3l10-5",t:"u3l10",q:"After a purge run, the signed list has 90,000 studies but 92,000 were deleted. What should you do?",a:["Stop and investigate before any further run","Accept the result because more space was freed","Run the purge again to confirm the same count","Delete the log so the difference is not found"],c:0,e:"The deleted count must match the signed list. A difference means something outside the approval was deleted, so stop and investigate."},

// u3l11
{id:"u3l11-1",t:"u3l11",q:"A wound care nurse types patient names in a photo app and three photos land under a misspelled name. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Ask the nurses to type each name more carefully in future and to check the spelling with the patient","Select the patient from the day's encounters or scan the wristband instead of typing","Stop taking photos until a new policy is written","Print the photos and file them in the paper chart"],c:1,e:"Encounter-based capture needs a fast way to select the patient and encounter with no typing."},
{id:"u3l11-2",t:"u3l11",q:"Which BEST describes orders-based imaging?",a:["Imaging that is never stored in any archive","Imaging captured at a bedside visit with no order","Imaging that follows a scheduled order and the modality worklist","Imaging performed only for research studies"],c:2,e:"Orders-based imaging follows an order and a worklist, as in radiology. Encounter-based imaging is tied to a visit."},
{id:"u3l11-3",t:"u3l11",q:"What does a vendor neutral archive provide in enterprise imaging?",a:["A change to the DICOM standard that every vendor must follow at the next release","A faster scanner for each department that uses it","A smaller monitor for each clinic workstation","Images from many systems in standard form with one patient identity"],c:3,e:"The VNA stores images from all departments in standard form so replacing one department's system does not strand its images."},
{id:"u3l11-4",t:"u3l11",q:"Which is the MOST common problem in visible light imaging?",a:["Patient identity and metadata","Storage speed","Screen resolution of the capture device","Color accuracy"],c:0,e:"Photos with no order and typed names produce identity problems. Without metadata an image cannot be found."},
{id:"u3l11-5",t:"u3l11",q:"Who decides required metadata, who may capture, and who may view in enterprise imaging?",a:["The vendor of the capture application","A governance group with department leads, compliance, and informatics","Each clinician for the images they take","IT alone, because it runs the archive"],c:1,e:"Governance assigns owners for image types, access, metadata, retention, and standards."}
);
